<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">M. Visvesvaraya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mokshagundam Visvesvaraya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">M. Visvesvaraya was an Indian civil engineer and statesman who served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> from 1912 to 1918. He designed flood-control and irrigation works across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, directed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-raja-sagara">Krishna Raja Sagara</a> dam and promoted industries, banks and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-mysore">University of Mysore</a>. He received the Bharat Ratna in 1955, and his birthday, 15 September, is celebrated as Engineers' Day in India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/m-visvesvaraya.jpg" alt="M. Visvesvaraya" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vishveshvarayya_in_his_30%27s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mokshagundam Visvesvaraya was born on 15 September 1861 at Muddenahalli in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, now in Chikkaballapura district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, into a Telugu speaking family. His ancestors came from Mokshagundam, a village in present-day Prakasam district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, and had migrated to Mysore before his birth. He was educated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a>, took a Bachelor of Science at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, and graduated in civil engineering from the College of Engineering, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>. There he helped found the Deccan Club and was its first secretary, knowing progressives of Pune such as R. G. Bhandarkar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gopal-krishna-gokhale">Gopal Krishna Gokhale</a> and Mahadev Govind Ranade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He became an assistant engineer in the Public Works Department of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> in 1885 and worked in the Bombay Presidency. Invited to join the Indian Irrigation Commission in 1899, he introduced an irrigation system on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a> and designed and patented automatic weir floodgates, first installed in 1903 at the Khadakvasla Dam near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> and later at the Tigra Dam in Gwalior and the Krishna Raja Sagar dam in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>. Around 1906 or 1907 he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> to study water supply and drainage, and his project was carried out there.</p><p>After retiring from British service he toured industrial countries, worked for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a> on flood protection against the Musi river, and in 1909 became chief engineer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, where he directed the Krishna Raja Sagar dam.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1912 Maharaja Krishnaraja Wadiyar IV appointed him Dewan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, an office he held for nearly seven years until 1918. With the maharaja's support he founded factories and institutions, among them the Mysore Soap Factory, the Mysore Iron and Steel Works at Bhadravathi, the State Bank of Mysore, the Mysore Chamber of Commerce and, in 1917, the Government Engineering College at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a>. He commissioned new railway lines and encouraged private investment in industry.</p><p>He was made a Companion of the Order of the Indian Empire in 1911, knighted in 1915, and awarded the Bharat Ratna in 1955. He wrote Reconstructing India (1920) and Planned Economy for India (1936), and died in April 1962 aged 100. His birthday is observed as Engineer's Day.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “M. Visvesvaraya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Krishnaraja Wodeyar IV (ruled 1894–1940, with full powers from 1902) and diwans such as <b>M. Visvesvaraya</b>, Mysore built the Shivanasamudra hydroelectric plant, the Krishna Raja Sagara dam, the University of Mysore and industries in Bangalore, which also hosted the Indian Institute of Science.</blockquote><a href="/state/mysore-state">Mysore State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Muddenahalli, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.41,77.69&amp;year=1926" aria-label="Muddenahalli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6680 1664 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of M. Visvesvaraya"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7130 1934h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7130 1934h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.41,77.69&amp;year=1926">What was here in 1926? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
